\title{stow: Durable File Downloads and Caching}
\description{
\code{stow} turns a remote file URL into a durable local path. It downloads the
file when needed and reuses a matching cached copy on later calls, including
across R sessions. Cached files live in a platform-appropriate user data
directory, so they do not depend on the current working directory or a
package installation.
}
\details{
The default \code{"stow"} cache namespace works for direct use. Package and
project authors can supply their own namespace and optional subdirectories
to keep unrelated files separate.
}
\section{Main functions}{

\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=stow]{stow()}} downloads a file or returns a matching cached copy.
\item \code{\link[=stow_path]{stow_path()}} locates or creates a cache directory.
\item \code{\link[=stow_info]{stow_info()}} lists the files in a cache directory.
\item \code{\link[=stow_prune]{stow_prune()}} removes outdated variants and orphaned internal files while
retaining current usable entries.
\item \code{\link[=stow_remove]{stow_remove()}} explicitly evicts all entries for one URL.
}
}

\section{Versions, validation, and durability}{

ETags can distinguish versions served from the same URL, and offline mode
can reuse a previously cached copy without a network request from \code{stow()}.
Earlier variants remain available until they are explicitly managed with
\code{\link[=stow_prune]{stow_prune()}} or \code{\link[=stow_remove]{stow_remove()}}.
Downloads are staged before they enter the cache. An optional validator can
reject both cached and newly downloaded content; a failed download or a new
file that fails validation is never committed as a cache entry.
}

